                <pb facs="0373" n="273" sameAs="frus:frus1880/pg273"/>

                <head>No. 170.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Seward to Mr. Evarts.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1880-05-31"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 31, 1880</date>.
                        (Received July 13.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 698.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to my dispatches, Nos. 650, 653,
                    and 661, in which I informed you that the diplomatic body had requested me to
                    speak with the ministers of the foreign office in regard to the mixed court at
                    Shanghai, and more particularly to the dispatch last named, in which a minute of
                    the interview was forwarded, I have now the honor to hand you herewith a copy of
                    a letter which I have received from Sir Thomas Wade, doyen of the diplomatic
                    body, in reference to the action taken by me.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>GEORGE F. SEWARD.</signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d170-a1.xml" xpointer="d170-a1"/>
